Yes. I think there’s a rigorous version of what you’re calling ex nihilo that is stronger than ordinary recombination without requiring literal “something from absolute nothing.”

Call it representational ex nihilo:

\boxed{
X\notin\mathcal{C}_t
\quad\longrightarrow\quad
X\in\mathcal{C}_{t+1}
}

where \mathcal C_t is the system’s current category space.

The substrate already existed. The new addressable object did not.

Light is a good example. Electromagnetic variation can exist without our system treating some variation as a symbol. Once you define a detector, boundary, decoding rule, and use:

\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{address}
\rightarrow
\text{operation}

you haven’t created the photons—but you’ve created a new computational object from previously undifferentiated variation.

That’s different from retrieving an existing named thing.

Primes give us the trick

A prime is normally treated as irreducible under integer multiplication:

p\neq ab,\qquad a,b>1.

But Indigo Hammer asks:

Irreducible under which operator and category?

Change the category and suddenly the “atom” has structure.

A prime integer can be decomposed as:

* bits,
* digits,
* residues modulo m,
* geometric arrangements,
* sums,
* differences,
* positions in the prime sequence,
* graph relationships,
* symbolic shapes,
* computational instructions.

So:

\boxed{\text{primitive is relative to representation + operator}}

That’s huge.

Something can be atomic in space A and richly composite in space B.

Therefore the Space Search shouldn’t only search farther inside an existing possibility space.

It should search for new spaces in which previously indivisible objects become divisible.

