That’s the summoning loop

Suppose the current ontology contains:

\mathcal C_0=\{A,B,C\}.

Ordinary creativity searches combinations:

A+B,\quad A+C,\quad B+C,\ldots

Space Search instead asks:

What transformation would make distinctions visible that A/B/C cannot currently express?

Generate representation R':

R:\;x\mapsto(A,B,C)

becomes:

R':\;x\mapsto(A,B,C,\color{gray}{?})

You observe repeated unexplained structure in ?.

Rather than force it into A,B,C:

?\rightarrow D.

Now:

\mathcal C_1=
\{A,B,C,D\}.

But D changes subsequent search because now you can construct:

A+D,\quad B+D,\quad D(D),\quad D^{-1},
\quad D\circ A,\ldots

One categorical invention causes a combinatorial explosion of previously impossible expressions.

That’s genuine search-space expansion:

|\Omega_{t+1}| \gg |\Omega_t|.

And if D itself provides a new composition operator, the growth can be much stronger than merely adding another item.

